WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.820
What a child experiences

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:04.720
during the first years of their life

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.820
has a lasting impact on the development of their brain.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.040
While genes set the roadmap,

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.480
experiences create the neural connection

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.480
inside the brain

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.980
that sets the foundations of our emotions,

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:20.060
language, motor skills, vision, and memories.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.780
If one region gets additional stimulation,

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.900
the neural pathways within that area

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.800
and the connections to other areas grow stronger.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.040
This process is called synaptic pruning.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.340
You can also imagine your brain as a planet.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.500
There is Motor Skill Metropolis,

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.540
Memory Mountain and Vision Village.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:41.520
Through the years,

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.220
popular cities grow bigger

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.480
and links between them get larger.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.900
Now if one area was never developed,

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.500
there can be traffic jams.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.960
This then slows down the development of the entire brain.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.360
Some neurotransmitters will be tired getting to work.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.660
Others will take short cuts and get lost.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:04.060
Betty Hart and Todd Risley

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.720
studied children’s exposure to language.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.400
On average children from families on welfare

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.460
were exposed to about 600 words per hour.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:15.700
Kids from rich families got about 2,000.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.900
By the age of 3, the gap becomes 30 million words.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.640
But it didn’t end there.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.440
Children from privileged families

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:26.020
received much more positive feedback.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.180
For every 6 words of praise,

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.640
there was only one word of discouragement.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.360
Welfare kids however,

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.220
heard twice as much discouragements than praise.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.900
This could make a difference that lasts a lifetime.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.120
In a study that began in 1963,

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.540
psychologist David Weikart and his team

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.560
randomly divided 123 underprivileged kids into two groups.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.080
One group spent two years at a top preschool with excellent teachers.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.400
They made art, discussed problems,

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.840
and received a lot attention, respect and love.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.800
For the other, life went on as usual.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:06.380
Often without much attention from anyone

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:11.140
40 years later the Highscope Perry Preschool Study was published.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.040
At age five, 67% of the children in the top preschool group

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.080
had an IQ of over 90,

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.880
they were school-ready.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.340
Of the others, only 28% achieved that.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:26.720
At fourteen there was a big difference in

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.640
basic classroom achievements.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.180
At twenty-seven the top preschool group

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.420
were more likely to own their own home.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.720
And at age forty they earned more money

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.900
and were less likely to ever be sentenced to jail.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:43.420
The researchers concluded that the two years at preschool

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.940
nourished the children not only intellectually

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:50.220
but also gave them social skills, courage and perseverance.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.380
This combination of character strength,

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:53.900
also called Grit,

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.760
was later responsible for their success in life.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:01.240
The 15,000 dollars invested in putting those kids into preschool,

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.740
later benefited the entire society,

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:05.920
mainly through a reduction in crime.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.800
The total return of investment

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:12.020
was estimated to be at a 195,000 dollars.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.500
In 2006 Nobel Prize winning economist James Heckman

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:20.420
published what became known as the Heckman Curve.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:23.640
It shows the return of investments in education,

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.940
which is the highest in the early years.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.560
Governments have since started to act.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:31.160
In Germany parents get a lot of financial

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.120
support to raise their kids.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:37.900
In Japan, mothers or fathers can take a full year of paid leave.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:42.640
In France, all children go to Kindergarten free of charge.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.840
If you happen to be in charge,

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.180
know that with every extra minute you spend

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.100
encouraging and talking to that little troublemaker,

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.560
you might be doing him a favour for life.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:56.220
According to the University of Michigan:

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:02.100
"regular family dinners are a stronger predictor of good grades than doing homework."

